#### INTERVENTION 3.2: Heart Failure Specialist Nurse-Led Community Management

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Mechanism:** Dedicated heart failure nurse specialists conduct medication titration, weight monitoring, remote symptom surveillance, and patient education, enabling early detection and treatment of decompensation before hospital admission is required. The "Hospital Avoidance" model is central.

**Resources required:**
- *Medium*: Heart failure nurse specialists; remote monitoring technology; MDT infrastructure with cardiologist oversight
- Requires: Community cardiology/heart failure service commissioning

**Implementation requirements:**
- Heart failure nurse specialist posts (NICE-recommended)
- Remote monitoring protocol (daily weight, fluid status, symptom reporting)
- Clear escalation pathway to acute cardiology
- Medication titration protocols

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Strong*: McAlister et al. (2004) meta-analysis (*Journal of the American College of Cardiology*, 29 RCTs) demonstrated specialised heart failure disease management programmes reduce all-cause mortality and hospitalisation
- NICE guideline NG106 (chronic heart failure, 2018) recommends specialist HF nurse involvement for all patients [governmental/NICE]
- British Society of Heart Failure (BSH) national audit data demonstrate wide variation in nurse-led service provision across England [professional body — noted as other source]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — services exist but are underdeveloped in many areas; requires targeted investment

**Barriers:**
- Heart failure nurse specialist workforce shortage
- Variable community cardiology commissioning
- Patient engagement with remote monitoring

**Metrics:**
- Heart failure emergency readmission rates
- % of heart failure patients on optimised guideline-directed medical therapy (GDMT)
- Time from symptom onset to specialist contact
- 30-day and 1-year mortality post-hospitalisation

