### PRIORITY 7: Delirium Prevention and Management

---

#### INTERVENTION 7.1: HELP (Hospital Elder Life Program) Multicomponent Delirium Prevention Bundle

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Mechanism:** HELP involves trained volunteers and nursing staff implementing non-pharmacological delirium prevention protocols: cognitive orientation (orientation board, familiar objects, regular conversation), mobility (daily walking programme), hydration and nutrition, sleep optimisation (non-pharmacological sleep protocol), and sensory aids (glasses, hearing aids at bedside). Addresses multiple simultaneous delirium risk factors.

**Resources required:**
- *Low–Medium*: Volunteer programme (significant time investment to train and coordinate); nursing staff training; orientation boards, sensory aid storage; sleep protocol implementation (non-pharmacological)
- One of the most cost-effective complex interventions per NICE CG103

**Implementation requirements:**
- Trust-wide HELP programme implementation with volunteer coordinator
- Staff training in delirium recognition (4AT tool) and prevention
- Ward environment assessment and modification
- Family engagement protocol (visitors as delirium prevention partners)
- Day/night routine standardisation on wards

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Strong*: Inouye et al. (1999) original HELP RCT (*New England Journal of Medicine*) — 40% relative risk reduction in delirium incidence
- Hshieh et al. (2015) — HELP meta-analysis (*JAMA Internal Medicine*) — 53% reduction in delirium odds
- NICE guideline CG103 (delirium, 2010, updated 2019) [governmental/NICE]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — training-led, low capital cost; volunteer recruitment can be challenging

**Barriers:**
- Volunteer recruitment and retention
- Staff buyin and ward culture
- High bed occupancy preventing adequate mobilisation of patients
- Night shift implementation of sleep protocols

**Metrics:**
- Delirium incidence rate on implemented wards
- % of patients ≥65 screened with 4AT on admission and at 72 hours
- Restraint and antipsychotic prescribing rates (proxy for delirium severity)
- LOS for delirious versus non-delirious patients (case-mix adjusted)

---

#### INTERVENTION 7.2: 4AT Delirium Screening Tool — Universal Implementation at Admission and Daily Monitoring

**Implementation level:** Clinical

**Mechanism:** The 4AT (Alertness, Abbreviated Mental Test-4, Attention, Acute change) tool is a validated 4-item screening tool for delirium that takes under 2 minutes to administer and has high sensitivity and specificity. Universal application at admission and daily thereafter enables early identification and timely non-pharmacological management.

**Resources required:**
- *Low*: Training (available via online NHS module); EPR documentation field; laminated bedside prompt cards
- Negligible capital cost

**Implementation requirements:**
- Mandatory 4AT documentation on EPR at admission
- Training embedded in junior doctor and nursing induction
- Clear escalation pathway when 4AT positive
- Documented daily reassessment during admission

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Strong*: Shenkin et al. (2019) — 4AT validation systematic review (*BMJ*, peer-reviewed, >3,000 patients) demonstrating sensitivity 88%, specificity 88% for delirium
- NHS Patient Safety Incident Response Framework (PSIRF) identifies delirium as a key harm area [governmental]
- NICE CG103 recommends delirium assessment tools [governmental/NICE]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — immediate implementability; no capital cost; major gains from standardisation

**Barriers:**
- Clinical culture ("delirium is expected in old patients" — nihilistic attitudes)
- Time pressure for nursing staff
- EPR documentation compliance

**Metrics:**
- % of admissions ≥65 with documented 4AT score on admission
- Time from positive 4AT to clinical review
- Rate of missed delirium diagnoses (identified retrospectively)
- New delirium diagnoses in-hospital (incident delirium)

---

#### INTERVENTION 7.3: Pharmacist-Led Delirium Risk Medication Review — Anticholinergic Burden Assessment

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Mechanism:** Anticholinergic drugs (including many antihistamines, bladder medications, antidepressants, and antipsychotics) independently increase delirium risk. Calculating the anticholinergic burden score (ACB scale) for every older patient at admission and reducing or substituting high-burden medications prevents iatrogenic delirium.

**Resources required:**
- *Low–Medium*: Ward pharmacist time; ACB calculator tool; prescribing guidelines
- Standard pharmacy cost

**Implementation requirements:**
- ACB scale embedded in EPR medication reconciliation workflow
- Pharmacist alert protocol for high ACB scores
- Prescribing guidelines for anticholinergic alternatives
- Medical team education on anticholinergic burden

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Moderate–Strong*: Vivian-Taylor and Hickey (2014) — anticholinergic burden associated with increased delirium risk in hospitalised older adults (*Maturitas*, peer-reviewed)
- Salahudeen et al. (2015) systematic review linking anticholinergic burden to adverse outcomes including delirium (*British Journal of Clinical Pharmacology*, peer-reviewed)
- STOPP/START Version 3 (O'Mahony et al., 2023) includes anticholinergic burden as a key prescribing safety indicator

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — pharmacist tool integration achievable; requires pharmacist availability

**Barriers:**
- Complex multimorbidity may limit substitution options
- Communication between prescribing teams
- Pharmacist workforce capacity

**Metrics:**
- Mean ACB score on admission in patients ≥65
- Rate of anticholinergic medication deprescribing on wards
- Correlation between ACB score reduction and delirium incidence

