### PRIORITY 8: Respiratory Surge Preparedness

---

#### INTERVENTION 8.1: Predictive Surveillance Modelling with NHS Real-Time Occupancy and Escalation Framework

**Implementation level:** Organisational / Policy

**Mechanism:** Using UKHSA respiratory surveillance data, NHS Situation Report data, and regional hospital occupancy data, ICS-level predictive models forecast surge timing and magnitude 1–2 weeks ahead. This triggers pre-emptive escalation: elective cancellation, discharge acceleration, mutual aid agreements, and staffing escalation before crisis point is reached.

**Resources required:**
- *Medium*: Data analytics capability within ICS; NHS England Situation Report infrastructure; UKHSA surveillance data feeds; escalation communication protocols
- Requires: Data sharing agreements, analytical capacity, escalation governance

**Implementation requirements:**
- ICS analytical team with respiratory surveillance modelling capability
- Defined escalation trigger thresholds (linked to NHSE Operational Pressures Escalation Levels [OPEL] framework)
- Pre-agreed surge response actions at each OPEL level
- Mutual aid agreement between trusts for surge staffing and patient transfer

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Moderate*: Hick et al. (2020) — healthcare surge capacity review (*Disaster Medicine and Public Health Preparedness*, peer-reviewed) provides framework for surge planning; evidence specific to UK winter NHS context is largely governmental/operational
- NHS England OPEL Framework (2022) and NHS England Winter Plan [governmental]
- UKHSA respiratory surveillance (weekly) and COVID-19 hospital occupancy data [governmental]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — surveillance infrastructure exists; analytical capacity at ICS level is variable

**Barriers:**
- ICS analytical workforce variation
- Real-time data quality (NHS SitRep data completeness)
- Political resistance to early elective cancellation

**Metrics:**
- Lead time from OPEL escalation trigger to surge peak (proxy for predictive accuracy)
- Days at OPEL 4 (most severe) during winter per year
- Bed occupancy rate at time of surge escalation activation

---

#### INTERVENTION 8.2: Respiratory Care Bundles for Community Management of Mild-to-Moderate COVID-19 and Influenza

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Mechanism:** Standardised community assessment pathways (including remote oximetry monitoring for COVID-19) with clear escalation criteria prevent delayed recognition of deterioration while avoiding unnecessary hospital admissions for clinically stable patients. NHS Pulse Oximetry@Home and COVID-19 Virtual Ward models are examples.

**Resources required:**
- *Low–Medium*: Pulse oximeters (per household or GP loan); NHS 111 enhanced protocols; GP safety-netting call protocols
- Significant logistical infrastructure required at scale

**Implementation requirements:**
- Pulse oximetry supply chain
- NHS 111 enhanced assessment protocols
- GP remote monitoring protocols
- Clear escalation thresholds (SpO2 <94% on air triggers urgent review)
- Patient education on oximetry use

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Moderate*: NHS England Pulse Oximetry@Home Programme evaluation (Sherwood et al., 2021, NHSE internal analysis) [governmental]
- O'Carroll et al. (2021) — remote monitoring of COVID-19 patients review (*BJGP Open*, peer-reviewed) — emerging evidence of safety and acceptability
- NICE COVID-19 rapid guidelines (2022) [governmental/NICE]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — COVID-19 experience has established infrastructure; needs maintenance and extension to influenza/RSV

**Barriers:**
- Patient digital literacy for remote oximetry reporting
- Clinical response capacity when deterioration detected
- Equity of access to technology

**Metrics:**
- Community COVID/influenza patients enrolled in remote monitoring
- Rate of escalation to hospital from community monitoring
- Serious adverse events in remotely monitored community patients
- Patient experience measures

