### PRIORITY 9: Care Home Quality and NHS Clinical Support (Enhanced Health in Care Homes)

---

#### INTERVENTION 9.1: Named Clinical Lead (GP/ACP) for Every Care Home with Weekly Multidisciplinary Visiting

**Implementation level:** Organisational / Policy

**Mechanism:** Every care home has a named GP or Advanced Care Practitioner (ACP) lead who conducts weekly structured visits, proactive reviews of deteriorating residents, medication reviews, and anticipatory care planning. This reduces the reactive 999 call pattern that drives avoidable emergency admissions.

**Resources required:**
- *Medium–High*: GP/ACP time; PCN-level care home visiting rota; MDT support (pharmacist, OT, dietitian)
- NHS England EHCH framework partially funds this through PCN contracts

**Implementation requirements:**
- PCN care home lead GP/ACP designation
- Weekly visit protocol and documentation standard
- MDT meeting structure with care home staff
- Anticipatory care planning (ReSPECT) integration
- Communication with ambulance service (escalation care plans flagged)

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Moderate*: NHS England EHCH pilot evaluation (NHS England, 2020, 2022) [governmental] demonstrated reduced emergency admissions in pilot sites
- Steves et al. (2012) — care home residents in hospital have disproportionately poor outcomes; supporting in-situ management (*Age and Ageing*, peer-reviewed)
- Implementation Science literature on EHCH: Goodman et al. (2019) — qualitative evidence of implementation variation (*BMC Health Services Research*, peer-reviewed)

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — PCN infrastructure exists; consistency and quality of implementation are major gaps

**Barriers:**
- GP workforce capacity (care home visiting competes with core primary care)
- Care home staffing turnover creates continuity challenges
- Variable care home quality and capacity for MDT engagement

**Metrics:**
- % of care homes with named clinical lead visit at least weekly
- Emergency admissions per care home resident per year
- 999 calls from care homes per 1,000 resident-months
- Anticipatory care plan completion rate for care home residents

---

#### INTERVENTION 9.2: Pharmacist-Led Annual Medication Reviews for All Care Home Residents

**Implementation level:** Clinical / Organisational

**Mechanism:** Given that care home residents have median 8–11 concurrent medications, regular structured pharmacist-led review using STOPP/START criteria reduces ADRs, inappropriate psychotropic prescribing, and falls risk, all of which generate emergency admissions.

**Resources required:**
- *Medium*: Clinical pharmacist time (PCN pharmacist visiting); remote GP oversight; care home medication administration record (MAR) access
- Requires: PCN pharmacist time allocation

**Implementation requirements:**
- Annual structured review minimum (quarterly for highest complexity)
- Pharmacist access to full medication history and MAR
- GP co-signature for recommended changes
- Documentation sharing with care home

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Strong*: Cooper et al. (2015) — pharmacist review in care homes reduces drug-related problems (*Research in Social and Administrative Pharmacy*, peer-reviewed)
- NICE guideline NG56 and NG5 support medication review in polypharmacy in care homes [governmental/NICE]
- NHS RightCare frailty scenario analysis identifies medication review in care homes as high-value intervention [governmental]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — PCN pharmacist structure supports this; needs explicit contractual requirement

**Barriers:**
- Pharmacist workforce capacity
- GP engagement with pharmacist recommendations
- Care home information systems variability

**Metrics:**
- % of care home residents with annual pharmacist medication review
- Medication-related hospital admissions from care homes
- Potentially inappropriate prescribing rates in care home residents
- Psychotropic prescribing rates in dementia patients in care homes

---

#### INTERVENTION 9.3: Telemedicine / Direct Clinician-to-Care-Home Advice Line

**Implementation level:** Organisational / Clinical

**Mechanism:** A direct advice line (telephone or video) staffed by a senior clinician (GP, geriatrician, or ACP) allows care home staff to access immediate clinical advice when a resident deteriorates, enabling treatment to begin in situ rather than defaulting to 999 call. Examples: NHS urgent care hub models, Scottish HARP programme.

**Resources required:**
- *Medium*: Staffing of advice line; video consultation technology; care home staff training in basic clinical assessment; integration with community urgent response teams
- NHS 111 can be adapted for this purpose

**Implementation requirements:**
- ICS-level clinical advice hub for care homes
- Community urgent response team to visit within 2 hours of clinical concern
- Care home staff training in NEWS2 and SBAR communication
- Clear escalation pathway from advice line to urgent community response

**Strength of evidence:**
- *Moderate*: Mason et al. (2018) — care home telemedicine review (*BMJ Open*, peer-reviewed) demonstrates reduced emergency admissions in multiple international settings
- NHS England EHCH Urgent Community Response specification [governmental]
- NHS Scotland HARP programme evaluation [governmental — Scottish context, noted]

**Feasibility:** Within 3 years — technology available; clinical staffing for advice line is the key challenge

**Barriers:**
- Care home staff clinical confidence (variable training levels)
- Advice line clinical staffing
- Integration with ambulance dispatch protocols

**Metrics:**
- Use rate of care home advice line (calls per 100 residents per month)
- Proportion of calls resulting in in-situ treatment versus hospital transfer
- Emergency admissions from care homes per 1,000 resident-months
- Response time from urgent community response team to care home

