## B. CROSS-CUTTING INTERVENTION STRATEGIES

The following strategies apply across multiple priorities and represent high-value investment areas:

---

### CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGY 1: Clinical Pharmacist Deployment Across Care Settings

Directly relevant to Priorities 2, 3, 6, 7, 9, 10

**Detail:** PCN-based and hospital-based clinical pharmacists are the single workforce group that can impact medication reconciliation (Priority 6), delirium prevention (Priority 7), COPD/heart failure/diabetes management (Priority 3), polypharmacy in care homes (Priority 9), and anticipatory care planning (Priority 2) simultaneously. NHS England's investment in PCN pharmacist posts represents the most effective cross-cutting workforce investment currently underway.

**Evidence:** NHS England Clinical Pharmacist Programme evaluation; STOPP/START criteria application; Mekonnen et al. (2016) reconciliation evidence.

---

### CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGY 2: 7-Day Multidisciplinary Geriatric Input

Directly relevant to Priorities 1, 2, 4, 7, 10

**Detail:** The single most consistent predictor of admission avoidance and safe discharge in frail older adults is access to geriatric MDT input (OT, physiotherapist, social worker, pharmacist, geriatrician) across 7 days. Weekend admission without MDT access is a well-documented driver of extended LOS. NHS trusts with 7-day geriatric cover demonstrate better outcomes.

**Evidence:** Ellis et al. (2011) Cochrane CGA review; NHS England 7-Day Services Standards; NHS Improvement data on weekend LOS variation.

---

### CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGY 3: Advanced Care Planning and ReSPECT Form Completion

Directly relevant to Priorities 1, 2, 9, 10

**Detail:** A patient with a completed ReSPECT form can have their wishes honoured at any point of NHS contact — ambulance, NHS 111, ED, or inpatient. The absence of this information leads to default resuscitation attempts, unwanted admissions, and ICU-level care for patients who would not want it. Systematic completion in frail, elderly, and palliative care populations prevents thousands of avoidable admissions annually.

**Evidence:** Brinkman-Stoppelenburg et al. (2014); NHS ReSPECT programme; NICE NG31.

---

### CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGY 4: Primary Care Network (PCN) Proactive Care Model

Directly relevant to Priorities 2, 3, 6, 9, 10

**Detail:** PCNs are the structural mechanism through which primary care can proactively manage high-risk patients. PCNs with active case-finding (eFI, risk stratification algorithms), structured MDT meetings, and named care coordinator functions can address ACSC prevention, frailty, medication safety, and care home quality simultaneously.

**Evidence:** NHS England PCN Network Contract DES; Clegg et al. (2016) eFI evidence; NHS Long Term Plan prevention mandate.

---

### CROSS-CUTTING STRATEGY 5: Health Literacy and Patient Activation Programmes

Directly relevant to Priorities 1, 2, 3, 6, 7

**Detail:** Patients with higher health literacy have better medication adherence, higher vaccination uptake, earlier healthcare-seeking behaviour, and better self-management of chronic disease. Investment in Patient Activation Measure (PAM) tools, health coaching, and social prescribing addresses a root cause of avoidable admissions across all condition areas.

**Evidence:** Hibbard and Greene (2013) — patient activation and outcomes (*Health Affairs*, peer-reviewed); NHS England Social Prescribing programme; NHS Patient Activation in Long Term Conditions programme.

