## F. EVIDENCE CLASSIFICATION SUMMARY

### Strong Evidence (Peer-reviewed systematic reviews/RCTs):
- CGA for frail older adults (Ellis et al., 2011)
- Influenza vaccination in high-risk groups (Jefferson et al., 2010)
- HELP delirium prevention (Inouye et al., 1999; Hshieh et al., 2015)
- Pulmonary rehabilitation in COPD (McCarthy et al., 2015)
- Heart failure nurse-led management (McAlister et al., 2004)
- STOPP/START medication review (O'Mahony et al., 2023; Patterson et al., 2014)
- Pharmacist medication reconciliation (Mekonnen et al., 2016)
- PESI-guided outpatient PE management (Aujesky et al., 2011)
- RSV vaccination efficacy (Walsh et al., 2023)
- Hospital at home for selected conditions (Shepperd et al., 2021)

### Moderate Evidence (Good evidence, implementation-dependent):
- SDEC systematic outcomes (Russ et al., 2015)
- D2A model (Shepperd et al., 2020)
- Care home telemedicine (Mason et al., 2018)
- Surge preparedness frameworks (Hick et al., 2020)
- Mental health liaison / RAID (Parsonage and Fossey, 2011)
- eFI risk stratification (Clegg et al., 2016)

### Mainly Case Examples / Observational:
- Real-time social care capacity platforms
- ICS-level integration outcomes
- NHS England virtual ward learning reports

### Promising but Undervalued:
- Frailty-aware SDEC (equity of SDEC access for older adults — undervalued because cross-specialty working is culturally challenging)
- Sick day rules (widely evidence-based but poorly implemented — undervalued due to low commercial interest and low visibility)
- Equity-focused virtual ward access protocols (undervalued because equity monitoring is not mandated in virtual ward deployment contracts)
- Anticholinergic burden reduction (undervalued because delirium is under-recognised as an admission driver)
- Community COPD telerehabilitation (undervalued because telerehabilitation lacks champion infrastructure and is perceived as "second-best")

